  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "title": "What eksctl builds",
      "items": [
        "By default it creates one VPC, a public and private subnet per AZ, and a NAT Gateway per AZ",
        "Load balancers go in the public subnets and worker nodes in the private ones",
        "It also applies the subnet tags (role/elb, role/internal-elb) for you"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "emerald",
      "title": "If you build it yourself",
      "items": [
        "At least two AZs, plus DNS hostnames and DNS resolution enabled, are mandatory",
        "A private subnet's 0.0.0.0/0 must point at the NAT Gateway in the same AZ",
        "To reuse an existing VPC, list the subnet IDs under vpc.subnets in the eksctl config"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "amber",
      "title": "Cost considerations",
      "items": [
        "A NAT Gateway per AZ raises availability but multiplies the hourly charge",
        "A single NAT Gateway (--vpc-nat-mode=Single) costs less but is exposed to AZ failure",
        "Routing ECR and S3 traffic through VPC endpoints cuts NAT processing charges"
      ]
    }
  ]
}